              (none)  active - in the group and forwarding
              (i)     inactive - in the group, not forwarding
              (x)     not candidate - configured, but cannot join yet; a
                      shut or link-down port cannot run LACP
            All three are members and all three are saved by Config/w.
  attached  the host NIC found on the other end, from the MAC table
    to      (blank until that end sends a frame - a down NIC never does)

"UP" IS NOT "IN USE". It describes the serdes, nothing more. A 10GBASE-KR
backplane link stays trained even when the host NIC at the far end is
administratively down, so a completely idle port reports UP forever while
the switch floods broadcast at it and gets nothing back. That is why "UP
idle" and the yellow are called out separately, and why the detail panel
shows rx/tx packet counts: counters moving is the only real proof of use.

IMPORTANT: after a cold bootstrap every gi port comes up SHUT and PoE off.
The switch boots but forwards nothing until you enable them. That is what
Cisco's switch_init.xml did, and we do not run it."""),

    ("VM NETWORKS", """\
TAB -> vnet is the one view that configures this HOST rather than the
switch. It exists because "which front jack does this VM come out of" is
two settings in two places, and getting one without the other is the most
common way for this to look broken.

NFVIS answered it with a network called lan-net. That network is a bridge
whose only physical port is the 10G backplane link (int-LAN = te2), plus a
VLAN on the ASIC deciding which jacks it reaches. The jacks themselves are
never bridge ports and are not host NICs at all.

swbr0 is that bridge, built the Proxmox way: VLAN-aware, with the X710
backplane NIC as its only port. Then:

  bridge=swbr0            VLAN 1 - every front jack, the stock lan-net
  bridge=swbr0,tag=100    whichever jacks you put in VLAN 100

  n       create swbr0. Prints the exact diff of /etc/network/interfaces
          first and writes nothing until you confirm it.
  D       remove it again. VMs go back to vmbr0. The SWITCH is left alone -
          front ports keep their VLANs and keep replaying from disk.
  ENTER   add or remove a front jack for the selected VLAN.

Two things this view will not do. It does not run 'ifreload -a' - nothing
it writes is live until you do, from a shell, ideally with the serial
console attached. And it changes no setting on vmbr0: that is Proxmox's own
management interface, the one link that does not depend on the ASIC, the
bootstrap VM or a VLAN, and it is how you get back in when this goes wrong.

The one mark it leaves there is a comment. n adds a line to the stanza of
whichever bridge carries the default route - found by looking, not by
assuming the name - saying guests belong on swbr0; Proxmox shows it as that
interface's comment. D removes it again. That is the cheap version of
renaming vmbr0, with nothing to migrate and nothing that can cost you the
interface you manage this host over.

Creating swbr0 MOVES sw2363 onto the bridge. That is required, not a
preference: once the backplane NIC is a bridge port, a VLAN subinterface of
the NIC itself receives nothing. If the switch stops answering after
ifreload, the VLAN device was not re-parented - 'ip link del sw2363' then
'ifreload -a' again.

The same thing from a shell, with more detail on each step:

  encs-switch-vnet status
  encs-switch-vnet init
  encs-switch-vnet add 100 --ports gi0
  encs-switch-vnet teardown"""),

    ("LINK AGGREGATION", """\
Select a gi port in the Ports view and press g. You are asked for a group
(1-4) and then a mode:

  auto  LACP - negotiates with the far end. Use this against any switch
        that runs LACP, and prefer it when unsure.
  on    static bundle - forwards immediately, negotiates nothing. Only
        correct when the far end is also static.

Pairing a static "on" bundle with a far end running LACP silently
black-holes traffic: neither side reports an error, frames just go missing.

Press g again on a member and answer 0 to take it out of the group.

  - only the 8 gi front ports can be bundled. te1/te2 are the internal
    backplane links that carry your traffic and this management session,
    so the TUI refuses to touch them.
  - the ASIC supports 4 groups (LAG1-4).
  - membership is as volatile as the rest of the config. Write it with
    the Config view or it is gone after a cold power cycle.

The write goes to the MEMBER PORT, not to the LAG: it sets LACPEnabled and
LAGID on the port's Standard802_3List entry, which is exactly what Cisco's
switch-confd sent for "channel-group"."""),

    ("SPANNING TREE", """\
THE SWITCH BOOTS WITH SPANNING TREE OFF. Nothing will break a cabling loop
until you turn it on, and a loop between two front ports takes the
management VLAN down with everything else.

TAB -> stp, then g -> enable. Two things to expect:

  - ports do not forward immediately. They go through listening and
    learning first, which with the default timers is about 30 seconds. A
    link that worked a moment ago going quiet is the protocol working.
  - a port with a server on it should have portfast set (ENTER ->
    portfast), or that server waits 30s for a link every time it reboots.
    Never set portfast on a port facing another switch.

The view shows per-port state and role as the switch reports them, and
auto-refreshes while they change.

Modes are STP, RSTP (the default, and what you want) and MSTP. Timers are
checked against 802.1D before they are sent: max_age must be at least
2*(hello+1) and no more than 2*(forward_delay-1). The switch enforces the
same rule but answers a violation with a bare FAILED.

Bridge priority is rounded down to a multiple of 4096 and port priority to
a multiple of 16, because the switch rejects anything else.

BPDU guard, root guard and link type are per-port under ENTER. Link type
is written to the RSTP table rather than STP - a detail that matters only
