    ("UPDATING THIS TOOL", """\
Version {ver}. On startup this tool asks GitHub once, in the background,
whether a newer release exists. If there is one the title bar says so; if
the host has no route to github.com nothing is reported and nothing breaks.
Set ENCS_NO_UPDATE_CHECK=1 to skip that request entirely.

The Config view's u key repeats the check on demand. Installing needs root
and happens from a shell:

  encs-switch-tui --check-update     does a newer release exist?
  encs-switch-tui --update           download and install it
  encs-switch-tui --version          what is installed right now

--update fetches the release tarball over verified HTTPS, checks it against
the published SHA256SUMS, refuses to install anything that does not compile,
saves what it replaces under /var/backups/encs-switch/<timestamp>/, and swaps
each file in atomically. It only ever writes to /usr/local/sbin,
/etc/systemd/system and /opt/encs-host.

It does NOT touch your switch config, the VLAN interface, or /etc/encs-switch.
Roll back by copying the files out of the backup directory.

On Proxmox it finishes by running 'encs-switch-vnet hostpci --fix' and
'encs-switch-vnet startup --fix': if a BIOS or CIMC update moved the Marvell
on the PCI bus, the bootstrap VM's hostpci line is re-pointed right away
instead of at the next boot - also when there was nothing new to install.
It never starts a VM - do that yourself once it says the address was fixed.
Coming from 0.2.1 or older, the copy doing the update predates this step, so
run --update a second time (or 'encs-switch-vnet hostpci --fix').

This updates the HOST tools only. The bootstrap VM's kernel, the Marvell
module and the firmware loader are baked into the image and are not
updatable this way - and must not be, since the module is pinned to one
exact kernel build."""),

    ("CONFIG IS VOLATILE", """\
The ASIC has no flash, so its configuration lives in RAM.

  VM restart / bootstrap service restart -> config SURVIVES
        (those do not remove power from the ASIC)
  cold power cycle / AC loss             -> config is LOST
        (comes back as VLAN 1 + 2363, all gi ports SHUT, PoE off)

So the files under {cfg} are the switch's real source of truth. Use the
Config view to write them, keep them in version control, and let
encs-switch-replay.service reapply them after a power loss.

What w writes, applied in filename order on replay. The first five are
always written; the rest appear only if you have actually configured that
area, so a plain L2 switch still has five files and not thirty:

  10-ports.xml       admin up/down per front port
  11-port-settings   description, speed, flow control
  15-lag.xml         LAG membership and mode
  20-vlans.xml       which VLANs exist, and their names
  25-vlan-ports.xml  which ports are in them, and how they tag
  30-poe.xml         PoE on/off per port
  35/36/37-stp-*     spanning tree: global, bridge timers, per port
  40-storm.xml       storm control rates
  41/42-span-*       mirror destination, then what it mirrors
  45/46/47-lldp,cdp  LLDP global and per port, CDP per port
  50/51-lacp-*       LACP system and port tuning
  55-mac-aging.xml   the aging timer, if it is not the default 300s
  56-static-mac.xml  static forwarding entries
  60/61/62-acl*      ACLs, then their rules, then their bindings
  65..68-qos*        QoS mode/trust, port CoS, CoS->queue, policers
  70-radius.xml      RADIUS servers      <- MODE 0600, see below
  71/72-dot1x-*      802.1X global and per port
  75/76/77-igmp*     multicast global, per-VLAN snooping, router ports
  80..83-pvlan*      private VLANs, associations, host and promiscuous ports
  84..88             routing on/off, gateway, routes, static ARP, ARP timeout

11-port-settings.xml is kept out of 10-ports.xml although both write
Standard802_3List: 10-ports.xml is what stands between a cold power cycle
and a switch that forwards nothing, and it has been replayed against real
hardware in exactly its current shape. A firmware that chokes on an
unfamiliar speedAdmin must not be able to take the port enables with it.

The numbering is dependency order, not decoration: an ACL rule replayed
before its ACL exists is rejected, and so is a binding to an ACL that is
not there yet. Same for RADIUS before 802.1X, and a private VLAN before
its associations.

ONE FILE IS ONE TABLE. Replay posts each file as a single request, and a
request body is one <DeviceConfiguration> around one table - which is why
an area that spans three tables is three files.

70-radius.xml CONTAINS THE SHARED SECRET IN CLEAR TEXT. The switch reports
keyString back in the clear and there is no way to replay a server without
it, so the file is created 0600 and {cfg} is set to 0700. Think before you
commit that directory to version control.

te1-te4 are deliberately NOT saved. te2 carries the management VLAN and
te1/te3/te4 are the module fabric the firmware sets up itself; replaying a
stale copy of those is a good way to cut your own session."""),

